      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "What is an ingestion job?",
      "category": "simple",
      "result": {
        "answer": "Based on the context, an ingestion job refers to a process that involves reading documents from a Git repository, processing them, and storing the processed data in a database or other storage system. This process can be triggered by various events, such as new commits being pushed to the repository or changes to existing files.\n\nIn the context of the Ecosystem-MCP microservices documentation system, an ingestion job is likely a task that runs in the background to collect and process data from multiple sources, such as Git repositories, into a centralized database. The job may involve tasks like:\n\n1. Cloning the Git repository\n2. Processing files (e.g., extracting metadata, parsing content)\n3. Storing processed data in a database or storage system\n4. Tracking progress and error handling\n\nThe Ingestion Job Repository (Source 9) provides CRUD operations for tracking these ingestion jobs, allowing developers to create, read, update, and delete job records. The Ingestion Service (Source 10) wraps the JobProcessor infrastructure and provides a clean interface for tests and API routes, making it easier to manage and coordinate ingestion jobs.",
